Aim:
To arrange components in five regions using BorderLayout in Java.

Algorithm:
1. Import the AWT and Applet packages.
2. Create a class named insertdemo that extends Applet.
3. Set the background colour to cyan.
4. Set the layout to BorderLayout.
5. Add buttons to the North, South, East and West regions.
6. Add a text area to the Center region.

Program:
import java.awt.*;
import java.applet.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/* <applet code="insertdemo" width=400 height=200>
</applet> */

public class insertdemo extends Applet {
    public void init() {
        setBackground(Color.cyan);
        setLayout(new BorderLayout());

        add(new Button("this is cross the top"), BorderLayout.NORTH);
        add(new Button("the footer messsage migth go here"), BorderLayout.SOUTH);
        add(new Button("RIGHT"), BorderLayout.EAST);
        add(new Button("LEFT"), BorderLayout.WEST);

        String msg = "the reasonable man adapts\n\n";
        add(new TextArea(msg), BorderLayout.CENTER);
    }

    public Insets getInsets() {
        return new Insets(10,10,10,10);
    }
}

Output:
A cyan applet displays buttons at the top, bottom, right and left, with a text area in the centre.

Result:
Thus, BorderLayout was implemented successfully in Java.

